# Workflow

Project type: `{{ project_type }}`. Runtime level: `{{ runtime_level }}`.
Governance: `{{ governance }}`. Workflow mode: `{{ workflow_mode }}`.

## Change Loop

{% if governance == "managed" %}
Understand enough -> build the smallest useful vertical slice -> run checks ->
review -> reconcile project state -> refine durable project knowledge.

Implementation can invalidate previous planning assumptions. Reconsider the
next-task selection after evidence is produced, and treat roadmap/status
documents as durable state descriptions, not immutable task queues.
{% else %}
Understand enough -> build the smallest useful vertical slice -> run checks ->
review -> update durable docs when the change teaches something durable.

If durable planning/status artifacts exist and this change affects them, keep
them truthful with the repository state. Lightweight projects do not require
task selection, next-task reporting, or a managed-style report footer.
{% endif %}

Use executable guardrails before process gates:

```bash
make setup
make check
make build
```

`make check` is validation-only. It must not rewrite project files. Use
`make format` for formatting changes.
{% if governance == "managed" %}
Use `make sync-project-docs` when generated project dashboards need refresh.
{% endif %}

## Agent Context

Load context in three tiers instead of reading the whole project up front:

1. Tier 0 bootstrap: repository rules and governance mode only (`AGENTS.md`,
   `project/brief.md`).
2. Tier 1 task/skill context: loaded deterministically for the actual
   operation, including the selected task, the selected skill's `reads:`,
   changed files, and narrowly relevant configuration.
3. Tier 2 exploration: directories listed as `search_roots` are search
   targets only. Open specific files when needed; never recursively load a
   directory into context.

{% if governance == "managed" %}
The deterministic context bundle is requested through Make:

```bash
make agent-context TASK=T-001
make agent-context TASK=T-001 SKILL=implement-change
make agent-context TASK=T-001 SKILL=review-change MODE=resume
```

{% endif %}
`.agents/context-map.yaml` separates explicit `files` (eagerly loaded
candidates) from `search_roots` (never loaded automatically) and declares a
deterministic `budget` (`max_files`, `max_bytes`). Context output reports
included files with categories, omitted files with reasons, available search
roots, and total bytes so regressions stay visible. Task, selected-skill, and
bootstrap files are protected from budget truncation.

New tasks use the default context mode. When resuming or fixing an existing PR,
use `MODE=resume`: the bundle prioritizes the task acceptance criteria, the
branch diff against the base, affected files, and the selected skill without
reloading the project's architectural context.

Prefer the focused checks recommended for the changed-file set while
implementing. Run the canonical full gate exactly once at final pre-review:

```bash
make check
```

`make check` is validation-only and already runs the project, documentation,
and agent-skill validators. Do not re-run the same validators separately
through `agent-pre-review`.

## Agent Skills

Skills are reusable agent capabilities, not a mandatory orchestration engine.
Use the skill that fits the situation.

Core skills are always generated:

- `orient-project`
- `implement-change`
- `verify-change`
- `review-change`
- `update-documentation`
- `create-adr`
- `conventional-commit`
- `capture-learning`

Lightweight projects use these skills with durable context only. Managed
projects use the same core skills and may add task context through managed
lifecycle commands.

{% if governance == "managed" %}
Managed lifecycle skills are generated under `.agents/managed/skills/` for
state assessment, task selection, task preparation, task completion, and project
reassessment. They control project-management process around core engineering
judgment.
{% endif %}

{% if project_type == "fullstack" or runtime_level == "production" %}
Capability skills are generated only when the selected profile needs them:
{% if project_type == "fullstack" %}

- `change-api-contract` for OpenAPI and generated client synchronization
{% endif %}
{% if runtime_level == "production" %}

- `verify-production-artifact` for build, inspect, readiness, smoke, and
  shutdown checks
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

`capture-learning` follows this escalation order:

```text
executable guardrail > durable documentation > agent instruction
```

Do not add textual rules when tests, validators, Make targets, or CI checks can
enforce the behavior.

## Governance

{% if governance == "lightweight" %}
Lightweight governance has no mandatory task state machine, approval state,
generated board, milestone gate, or ready-for-development gate. Keep durable
context small:

- `project/brief.md` for problem, users, outcome, constraints, and first slice
- `docs/architecture.md` for current technical shape
- `docs/decisions/` for ADRs when decisions become durable

Ordinary reversible implementation work can proceed when it fits that context
and `make check` passes.
{% else %}
Managed governance uses deterministic project state, task lifecycle commands,
dependencies, dashboards, and approval metadata.

Use the deterministic commands instead of editing task status by hand:

```bash
make agent-status
make agent-context TASK=T-001
make agent-context TASK=T-001 SKILL=implement-change
make agent-context TASK=T-001 SKILL=review-change MODE=resume
make agent-pre-task TASK=T-001
make project-status
make task-ready TASK=T-001
make task-start TASK=T-001
make agent-pre-review TASK=T-001
make task-review TASK=T-001
make task-complete TASK=T-001
make agent-post-task TASK=T-001
make task-block TASK=T-001 REASON="..." UNBLOCK="..."
make task-unblock TASK=T-001
make task-cancel TASK=T-001
make sync-project-docs
make validate-project
```

Allowed transitions are `backlog -> ready`, `ready -> in-progress`,
`in-progress -> review`, `in-progress -> blocked`, `review -> in-progress`,
`review -> done`, `blocked -> ready`, `blocked -> in-progress`, and cancellation
from any active state. A0 tasks may also move from `in-progress` directly to
`done` when Definition of Done is satisfied.
{% endif %}

## Human Approval

Human approval is based on blast radius, irreversibility, security, cost, and
production risk.

Human approval is required for:

- destructive or irreversible data operations
- authentication or authorization boundary changes
- security-sensitive changes or secret handling
- paid external services
- production deployment
- destructive schema migrations
- major scope expansion
- infrastructure with significant operational or financial consequences

Normal reversible implementation work does not need human approval merely
because it adds a module, endpoint, internal refactor, tests, UI behavior, or
ordinary public behavior required by already-scoped work.

{% if governance == "managed" %}
Managed approval levels:

- A0: no human approval required; use `approval_status: not-required`
- A1: implementation can proceed, but `review -> done` requires human approval
- A2: work cannot start until a human has explicitly approved it

AI agents must not autonomously create human approvals. A human may record one
with:

```bash
make task-approve TASK=T-001 APPROVED_BY="Human Name"
```
{% endif %}

## Task Records

Task files are durable project records, not execution journals. Keep them
concise and forward-looking:

- `Goal`, `Context`, `Scope`, and `Out of Scope` explain why the work exists.
- `Acceptance Criteria` and `Verification` carry durable, reproducible
  evidence.
- `Documentation Impact` and `Completion Notes` capture the outcome.

Do not append chronological execution logs, repeated command output, failed
intermediate attempts, CI play-by-play, or rebase history to task files. That
transient detail belongs in the commit history, the pull request description,
and review discussion.

## Git Workflow

{% if workflow_mode == "local" %}
`local` mode is suitable for experiments and personal projects. Agents may work
in the current local repository. Commits, pushes, and pull requests are optional
unless requested.
{% elif workflow_mode == "branch" %}
`branch` mode asks agents to work on a dedicated branch. Commits may be created,
but pushing and pull requests are optional unless requested.
{% else %}
`pr` mode preserves the strict branch -> commit -> push -> pull request
workflow. Agents work on a non-`main` branch, commit their own changes, push to
`origin`, and open a ready pull request. Direct pushes to `main` are not part of
this workflow.
{% endif %}

## Release and Post-Release

Local projects may stop at verified checks. Shared and production projects need
release notes, rollback notes, and post-release verification appropriate to
their runtime level.

{% if project_type == "fullstack" and runtime_level == "production" %}
For production full-stack projects, the minimum local release candidate check is:

```bash
make setup
make api-check
make check
make build
make image-build
make image-inspect
make prod-up
make prod-status
make prod-smoke
make e2e-production
make prod-down
```

Treat that as artifact readiness only. Actual deployment still needs a target
environment, secret handling, rollout and rollback ownership, and any required
human approval.
{% endif %}
